  "body": "<p>A voice beyond the tear in reality announced that its interrupted project could continue through a new route. The adventurers heard a delivery protocol; inside, the machines treated them as intruders to be exterminated.</p><p>The company fought for control of the platforms until getting the wounded out became more urgent than sealing the rift. Its ending includes a carried body, an argument over who should leave, and Ithilrûnë’s final stand.</p><h2 id=\"before-the-machinery\">A tear in the working day</h2><p>The opening could be seen from whichever island a traveler called home: a jagged, almost pyramidal rip with colors like thick paint running around its edges. Beyond it, brass tubes, cogs and gears moved to a rhythm of ticking, steam and heavy machinery. A flat voice announced an interrupted project. It had found a route into its destination realm and was starting a secondary delivery.</p><p>The people gathering at the breach had not yet made a company. Carmen proposed breaking the machines and selling them. Ithilrûnë needled her about staying on Prima, then warmly greeted Paco. Eldin tried to work out which of the strange figures were allies. Cecil suggested reserving the fight for the automatons. Brianna, introducing herself alongside Magnus, was plainly reluctant to enter.</p><p>Cecil told her she could retreat if she was not ready. She chose to continue. Evrelki arrived prepared to oppose an invasion, naming himself a Paladin of the Watchers. Magnus bowed in greeting and said he was ready. Whatever agreement they had reached outside, it would have to survive the machinery within.</p><h2 id=\"chapter-1\">The path into the machinery</h2><p>Two paths wound through arrangements of pipes, clocks and gears that alternated between apparent logic and impossible disorder. Cecil let his falling staff choose a direction. Ithilrûnë speculated that Death would favor rigid order, then distrusted the ordered-looking passage. Eldin preferred the apparent high ground. The group’s spoken intentions wandered between right and left before the recorded approach brought them along the left-hand path.</p><p>It opened onto an elevated platform above damaged stone walkways. Clock hands, coils, springs and broken parts lay scattered below. An unnatural blue light shone from the room’s center. Near it stood golden ram-like constructs with feathered wings and bladed humanoid machines.</p><p>Ithilrûnë wanted the flying creatures dealt with first, while the adventurers still held a height advantage. Magnus accepted her lead. Eldin hoped they could reseal the rift. Cecil placed a protective ward on Brianna. She had already admitted her unease at something built to imitate living nature.</p><p>The automatons saw them and began broadcasting an extermination order. One directed its message toward the central light. The machinery was communicating as well as moving. Evrelki tried to ask its purpose, but the encounter was becoming a battle before the company could learn much by conversation.</p><h2 id=\"chapter-2\">The defenses change</h2><p>Carmen offered to turn a companion into a tyrannosaur. Brianna could barely hear her across the battlefield; Cecil preferred to provide support. Before that offer produced an answer, a golden ram took flight and breathed fire over Cecil and Brianna. Another rushed Eldin, knocked him down and struck again while he lay on the ground.</p><p>Cecil repeated that Brianna could leave while the breach remained open. She took the chance when it came and vanished from the fight. Her departure was a decision she had been allowed to make, rather than a disappearance the others had to interpret as death.</p><p>Paco attacked a ram under Carmen’s impatient direction. When a machine burst apart in electricity, he celebrated as though he had brought it down himself. Ithilrûnë’s lightning damaged the surrounding enemies. The companions could hurt these defenses, but their successes did not stop replacements arriving.</p><p>A brass-and-silver support unit floated through the opening. It announced that magic had been detected and that it would preserve its lord’s delivery schedule. Evrelki went down as the constructs pressed forward. Fire proved ineffective against creatures that breathed it themselves. The support unit launched crystal darts at Carmen and announced that mages were to be eliminated.</p><p>Carmen pointed it toward Ithilrûnë instead. Ithilrûnë retorted that she was the one actually killing enemies. Their quarrel had become part of an immediate struggle over who would draw the machines’ attention.</p><h2 id=\"chapter-3\">A delivery for Death</h2><p>Then something larger rolled out of the rift: a grotesque union of flesh and machinery, with front legs, rear wheels, saws and bloodied spikes. It introduced itself as a collector sent by the Lord of Blades for Death. Its theatrical delivery speech gave the company a declared purpose for the invasion. It did not give them independent knowledge of every arrangement behind that claim.</p><p>The collector urged its allies to bring it more bodies. Magnus kept fighting with his black-and-gold magic, then his rapier. Evrelki regained consciousness and refused to abandon his oath to prevent invasions. When Carmen was attacked, he tried to shield her. Cecil was knocked down by another charging ram.</p><p>Ithilrûnë caught the collector in a Forcecage. For a moment she had taken the largest threat out of the immediate fighting. She told the others they could ignore it and use the time. The support unit answered by changing its priority to her, identifying a major magical threat.</p><p>The collector remained in its cage, encouraging the machines outside it. One contained enemy could still direct a fight. The golden ram struck Magnus and sent him crumpling to the floor, while another of Ithilrûnë’s spells failed to move the machine staring her down.</p><h2 id=\"chapter-4\">Getting the wounded out</h2><p>Ithilrûnë’s commands changed from choosing targets to getting people away. Anyone who could get back up was to flee. She made Magnus invisible, pushed him out of danger and put herself between him and the attack. The closing sequence records that protection; it does not show every step of his eventual exit.</p><p>Evrelki healed himself and offered to hold the machines back. Ithilrûnë rejected the offer and ordered him out too. The people trying to protect the retreat were struggling to persuade one another to take part in it. Staying behind could sound like duty even when there was no longer a workable defense to hold.</p><p>Weapons cut deeply into Evrelki. In his last words he apologized to Scribonia for failing to protect him, calling him his brother. His account leaves the once-bright mothlike body pale and still. Carmen, in dinosaur form, seized the limp moth boy and carried him out before her transformation ended. The rescue post does not name its passenger; it preserves the physical act of carrying someone who could not make the exit unaided.</p><p>As people withdrew, the collector screamed from its cage over the corpses it was losing. Its plan had not ended in the clean collection it expected. That did not mean the company had conquered the machinery around it.</p><h2 id=\"the-last-cover\">Cecil’s answer</h2><p>Ithilrûnë had shouted at Cecil to leave, threatening him in her desperation. He answered the judgment beneath that demand: her life counted too.</p><div class=\"quote\">Your life isn&#x27;t any less valuable than the others. You need to leave, too!<cite>Cecil</cite></div><p>She turned toward him with a tear in her eye and repeated the order. Cecil faded from the scene. In her own closing account, Ithilrûnë fell under the accumulated darts and slashes after seeing him go. She smiled through the pain, believed they had lost this encounter but might win another day, and gave up her breath.</p><p>That fall belongs in the account of the retreat. The closing exchange supplies no completed extraction for her and no named spell returning her afterward. Her wider history cannot be used to make these last moments into an uncomplicated withdrawal in which everyone is shown safely home.</p><h2 id=\"chapter-5\">The outcome</h2><p>The company had entered hoping to investigate and close a breach. It left under pressure, having identified an organized delivery, fought changing defenses and trapped a collector without destroying the entire machine realm. Brianna’s exit, Carmen’s carried companion and Cecil’s disappearance are recorded individually. Evrelki’s wounds and Ithilrûnë’s last stand prevent that list from becoming a claim that the expedition suffered no losses.</p><p>Cecil’s instruction to spend an action leaving was permission within this encounter while the opening remained available. It was useful because people could still act on it. The scene also showed why a companion unable to move might need someone else to get them through.</p><p>The collector’s patrons remained names spoken by the collector. The breach’s final condition, the complete fate of its machinery and the exact subsequent returns of the fallen are not supplied by this ending. The fight closes on the survivors getting people out, and on the person who urged the last of them to leave.</p><p class=\"chronicle-context-links\"><strong>People, places and connected events</strong> <a href=\"#rifts\">Rifts</a> · <a href=\"#carmen\">Carmen</a> · <a href=\"#ithilrune\">Ithilrûnë Ailinor</a> · <a href=\"#brianna\">Brianna</a> · <a href=\"#magnus\">Magnus Niriin</a></p>",
